====================================================================================================================
VERDICT -- item 115
====================================================================================================================
  Renzo's rule DOES extend to second order, and the coefficient is the predicted one: beta[(1+n) L''] = 0.94 +- 0.13
  against a prediction of exactly 1.000, with the reconstruction residual falling 8% below the first order alone.  The
  framework beats Newton at both orders and beats deep-MOND-everywhere at second order.  Nothing here is fitted.

  Three things are reported against interest.  (i) The item's own criterion -- a pointwise curvature correlation above
  0.5 -- FAILS at 0.18, and a noise-ceiling simulation shows the threshold was unreachable with SPARC's error bars.
  (ii) The piece that is genuinely NEW at second order, ndot (L')^2 -- the kernel's log-log curvature -- is NOT detected:
  -0.13 +- 0.52 against a predicted 1.  Item 91 failed to measure the same quantity from binned RAR medians; this is an
  independent route to it and it fails too.  (iii) The estimator's preferred a_0 is 0.5 dex high and moves 0.5 dex with
  the smoothing window, so it is a slope meter and not a scale meter -- do not quote an a_0 from it.

  What the item asked -- does the second order add information beyond the first? -- answers YES for the (1+n) L'' term
  and NO for the ndot term.  The first is the first-order relation differentiated; the second is the only place a new
  kernel property enters, and it is below the noise.

